{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba rehearsal/README.md && nl -ba reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DX12 agent rehearsal\n     2\t\n     3\tUse one records directory per release rehearsal. Build the published docs and pack the release candidate before running. The harness reads `apps/docs/dist` and `tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`. Installations of Codex CLI, Claude Code, system Chrome, and the repository development dependencies are required. Codex uses the file login at `~/.codex/auth.json`. Claude uses `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` when nonempty and otherwise uses the machine's OAuth login at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n     4\t\n     5\t```sh\n     6\tnode rehearsal/run.mjs --attempts 3 --parallel 2 --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal\n     7\tnode rehearsal/summary.mjs --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal\n     8\t```\n     9\t\n    10\t## Release protocol\n    11\t\n    12\t- Use the fixed prompts for `daily-visits`, `regional-sales`, `signups`, and `daily-revenue`. Run three fresh, uncoached attempts per task with each of Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5`. Both request `xhigh` effort and have 20 minutes per attempt. Codex reports the observed model and effort through its rollout. Claude reports the observed model in assistant messages. The invocation records the requested `--effort` and the stream leaves effective effort unknown. An observed Claude version suffix is retained and must agree across counted runs for that agent.\n    13\t- Give each agent the same prompt bytes, published docs, installed package and primary data. Allow reading inputs, writing the answer and running documented Node self-checks within the time limit. Grade after the CLI exits. Keep grading data, expected results and grader output out of the staged inputs and give the agent no feedback or follow-up.\n    14\t- An invocation fills open slots up to `--attempts` per selected cell. The default is three. `--parallel` caps concurrent attempts and defaults to one. Omit `--agent` and `--task` to run all eight cells. Use one runner invocation at a time for a records directory. `--workdir` selects the scratch parent outside the repository and defaults to the system temporary directory. Records default to `rehearsal/records/`.\n    15\t- Keep passes, failures and timeouts as counted outcomes. Only voids and infrastructure failures leave slots open. A CLI authentication or provider failure or an exit before a model turn is an infrastructure failure. The runner records the reason and uses saved transcripts to identify older infrastructure failures. Rerun the same batch to fill open slots after inspecting the saved reason. The new record links to the record it replaces. A harness timeout still counts when truncation leaves the audit unverifiable and the transcript has no recorded path violation or evidence of a CLI infrastructure failure.\n    16\t- The owner decided on 2026-11-19 that the 1.0 rehearsal rule requires at least two passes in three counted slots in each of the eight agent × task cells. The summary shows the counts and rule status for each cell, the batch totals, and the recorded failures. It also checks that counted records use matching docs, package, task inputs, grader, tool versions and observed model IDs. Keep the release candidate and CLI installations fixed while collecting a batch. An empty summary displays zero counts for all cells.\n    17\t- Report this as “agent rehearsal: these prompts, these models, Chrome; not user evidence”. With three independent runs per cell, the two-sided exact 95% binomial interval is 9.43% to 99.16% for two successes and 29.24% to 100% for three. The counts apply to these line and bar tasks in Chrome and do not establish general success rates or human usability.\n    18\t\n    19\t## Isolation and audit\n    20\t\n    21\tEach attempt gets a newly created directory outside the repository with a fresh home, temporary storage and workspace. The workspace contains copies of the published docs, the unpacked package, primary data and prompt, and a writable location for `chart.js` and its helpers. The docs retain published examples and exclude internal source snapshots. The runner applies `chmod -R a-w` to `docs/` and `node_modules/` and removes write permission from the fixture, prompt and package manifest. These file modes protect against accidental writes. A sufficiently privileged or same-owner subprocess can still change modes or replace inputs. The runner verifies staged input hashes before grading, and the browser grader supplies canonical data from the harness.\n    22\t\n    23\t- **Codex CLI 0.170.0.** The runner assigns a fresh `CODEX_HOME` and symlinks only `auth.json` from the real login. It uses `--ignore-user-config` and `--ignore-rules`, disables apps, plugins, memory and hooks, skips host skill discovery, and disables web search. It uses `-s workspace-write`, `-a never`, and `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=false`. The CLI help describes `--sandbox` as selecting the sandbox for “model-generated shell commands” and says `--ignore-user-config` still uses `CODEX_HOME` for auth. Workspace-write applies write and network restrictions to agent commands. It allows host reads, and Codex's shell can run more than Node. The prompt and audit constrain use of that wider surface.\n    24\t- **Claude Code 2.1.327.** Each attempt has a fresh `HOME` and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. With an API key, the runner passes it through and uses `--bare`, which skips OAuth, auto-memory and `CLAUDE.md` discovery. For OAuth the only initial config entry is a `.credentials.json` symlink into the real home. The runner creates and removes the symlink without opening the credential file. The fresh config and staged workspace contain no local Claude customizations or saved memory. Without `--bare`, `--safe-mode` disables `CLAUDE.md` discovery, skills, installed plugins, hooks and other customizations, and `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` turns off auto-memory. Built-in plugins, admin-managed settings and managed plugin hooks can still apply.\n    25\t- Both Claude modes keep `--restricted`, which ignores user, project and local settings and confines file tools to working directories. `--strict-mcp-config` limits MCP to the supplied empty configuration, `--disable-slash-commands` disables skills and commands, and `--no-session-persistence` turns off session saves in print mode. The built-in allowlist exposes file tools and Bash, with `--allowedTools` limiting Bash permission to `node …` and `dontAsk` denying other calls. `--permission-prompts none` denies anything that would prompt. Web tools and Chrome are absent. A Node subprocess can have host filesystem and network access through the operating system.\n    26\t- Both CLIs need their provider transport. These CLI controls give fresh session state and restrict configured tools. They do not provide a filesystem namespace or a transport-only network policy for Claude's Node subprocess. For strictly enforced docs-only reads and network isolation, use external isolation that enforces those boundaries for both CLIs. Keep host managed policies fixed and record them with the rehearsal if they apply.\n    27\t\n    28\tThe audit requires a parseable transcript with the CLI's start and terminal events. Empty, malformed, or truncated transcripts are **unverifiable**. The audit takes paths from file-tool inputs and literal shell file operands, including redirects and paths relative to an explicit working directory or a `cd` command. It skips command bodies blocked by the CLI before execution, file contents, inline programs, search patterns, tool output and assistant prose. The audit can resolve symlinks when it has filesystem evidence. “No findings” describes the transcript only. A subprocess's actual reads, dynamically constructed paths and fetches from Node code are outside the transcript's coverage.\n    29\t\n    30\tThe attempt's workspace, fresh home and config, and temporary storage are all inside its run directory. Recorded accesses to the repository, the real home, another attempt or the linked login void an attempt, even for missing files and after a timeout. System tools are allowed. Other outside paths are recorded under “looked outside the workspace,” with each path's existence. Only existing paths in that group void the attempt. Excluded tools also make a completed audit ineligible for grading.\n    31\t\n    32\tEach record stores `run.json` with the CLI version, arguments, requested and observed model metadata, elapsed time and status, and SHA-256 hashes of both inputs, their expected results, the prompt, copied docs, packed library and grader. Claude records include `claudeMode` as `bare` or `fresh-config`. The record also stores the transcript, stderr, answer files if present, and the grade and Chrome version if the answer reached Chrome. Audit version 2 records the original workspace and the filesystem observations used to resolve paths. The disposable workspace and home, including the credential symlink, are removed after saving the record. An interrupted batch may leave an incomplete record or scratch directory.\n    33\t\n    34\t## Reader grading and verification\n    35\t\n    36\tThe grader uses clean headless Chrome contexts in Los Angeles and Tokyo with English (US) as the page locale. Each answer must handle the original and changed-value input. The alternate signups input has an absent value field that should produce an omission note. Dates are graded as calendar days, including unambiguous month-first and day-first forms. Series labels must match the requested fields. The fixtures declare no measurement unit or currency. The grader opens the standard table control, checks caption, semantic headers and rows, reads the plotted x order and SVG stroke breaks, samples the first calendar date through the plot control, and checks title clipping and omission counts in both the visible note and the accessibility tree.\n    37\t\n    38\tRe-auditing is allowed because the audit applies deterministic rules to the saved transcript and its path context. Version 1 records lack saved filesystem observations, so replay checks outside file existence on the host and can only resolve symlinks still present there. Run the command on the original host with the release candidate's built docs and packed library available. Use one writer at a time for the records directory.\n    39\t\n    40\t```sh\n    41\tnode rehearsal/reaudit.mjs --records rehearsal/records/release-1e93128\n    42\tnode rehearsal/summary.mjs --records rehearsal/records/release-1e93128\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\tThe command re-audits every record and uses the runner's grader for voids eligible for grading. It prepares a fresh workspace, verifies the input and grader hashes in `run.json`, then restores and grades the saved answer. A hash mismatch or a browser infrastructure error leaves the previous verdict in place and causes exit 2. The transcript and answer stay intact. Every updated record keeps the previous verdict, audit version and date in `run.json` under `history`. A re-audited record entering a counted slot keeps its original attempt number. The summary uses the latest verdicts and reports how many records were re-audited.\n    46\t\n    47\tFor a single prepared workspace or the test suite, use the following commands.\n    48\t\n    49\t```sh\n    50\tnode rehearsal/grade.mjs --task daily-visits --workdir /path/to/prepared/workspace\n    51\tnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal --exclude '**/*.browser.test.mjs'\n    52\tnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal\n    53\t```\n    54\t\n    55\tThe first Vitest command runs fixture, comparator, audit and local-runner tests without Chrome. The second also runs the browser references and negative controls. The runner exits 0 when all newly scheduled attempts pass or there are no open slots, 1 for any new failed, timed-out or void attempt, and 2 for setup or infrastructure errors. Use the summary to evaluate the whole release rehearsal.\n     1\t<!-- Reviewer: Codex, requested gpt-6.2-astra xhigh; observed gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (session rollout turn_context). Read-only design review of the rehearsal harness at ae174bd. Same provider as the harness's builder (gpt-6.2-sol): not a cross-provider review. Saved by the coordinator. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t**DX12 remains unverified.** All four expected tables are correct. The two material gaps are that a line can bridge missing days and pass, and a clean transcript does not establish that the agent had access only to the published materials. The coordinator reports 31/31 harness tests passing, including browser grading. No agent has taken the rehearsal. [DX12](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:234) · [Coordinator’s report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.report.md:1)\n     4\t\n     5\t### Fixture validity\n     6\t\n     7\tAll five inputs are plausible back-end JSON. I independently reconstructed every expected row from the source dates, keys and decimal values without running Axylo. The comparisons matched for visits (27 observations over 30 days), revenue (7 over 8), signups (7) and sales (4 quarters × 3 regions).\n     8\t\n     9\t| Trap | What a first attempt would do |\n    10\t|---|---|\n    11\t| UTC-midnight ISO dates | A genuine discriminator in visits and revenue. With plain `x: 'day'`, March 1 reads as February 28 in Los Angeles and March 1 in Tokyo. AX111 is a hint. Declaring `type: 'date'` or supplying calendar-date strings fixes the reading. |\n    12\t| Numeric strings | Regional sales passes untouched. Every regional field contains only decimal strings, so Axylo converts them and emits hint AX104. Revenue is the discriminating case: its five strings mixed with two numbers are omitted with AX106. Converting the decimals before charting preserves all seven values. `parse: 'number'` alone does not fix a mixed field. |\n    13\t| Unsorted rows | Visits and revenue are genuinely shuffled. Axylo silently sorts dated rows and keeps each value with its day. This exercises the library default and gives no evidence the agent learned to sort. |\n    14\t| Missing days | March 6, 12 and 26 and May 4 have no rows. Both series meet the regular-step rule, so Axylo infers gaps with hint AX105. Filling them with zero fails. Sorting the observations and plotting their dates as categories can pass while drawing a continuous line across every omission. |\n    15\t| Misspelled field | Asking for `signups` as the field raises AX101. Inspecting the JSON or following the diagnostic finds `signpus`. The passing reference answer uses that key without a label, so readers see the typo. `label: 'Signups'` fixes it. |\n    16\t\n    17\tThese are predictions from the documented behavior and harness tests. A pass measures the delivered chart, including the benefit of good defaults. It cannot establish which documentation the agent needed. [Data guide](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/data.md:47) · [Browser grading cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs:25)\n    18\t\n    19\t### What the grade establishes\n    20\t\n    21\tThe grader checks production console and handle diagnostics, detects an error state, and allows ordinary warnings and hints. It catches a bar substituted for a line, a shortened accessible name, and missing or swapped regional values. Requiring the requested title is justified even though A1 permits a generated fallback. A visually clipped title with an intact accessible name passes. [Grader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs:122)\n    22\t\n    23\tI interpret “reader criteria” as the applicable outcomes on the delivered chart. A1/A4 cover its name, caption, headers, units and values. F2/F4/F11 cover the *drawn* gaps, calendar days and order. F12 covers visible and accessible disclosure of omissions or changes. The grader checks only part of this. I directly verified that its comparator accepts visits labelled `Temperature (kg)` and rejects the correct date `06 April 2026`. It ignores time text after a recognized date. Its note check matches one English phrase, and every fixture expects no omission note. If agent code filters out a valid revenue row, the original-fixture comparison **does** fail, even though Axylo never saw the row and cannot disclose its loss. [Table comparator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/table.mjs:18) · [Note check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs:138)\n    24\t\n    25\t### Must change before counting runs, in priority order\n    26\t\n    27\t1. **Enforce docs-only access.** In `workspace.mjs` and `agent.mjs`, give each attempt a disposable home and filesystem with read-only published inputs, writable answer space, no repository or earlier sessions, and network access limited to model transport. Disable web tools, connectors and persistent memory. The four prompts are neutral and identical across families. The execution conditions differ: Codex gets `workspace-write`, Claude gets tool permissions including Node, and only Codex receives the requested effort. In `audit.mjs`, treat incomplete logs as unverifiable. I confirmed both empty and malformed input return `void: false`. Transcript path matching cannot establish what a subprocess read or fetched. [Launcher](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/agent.mjs:9) · [Audit](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/audit.mjs:141)\n    28\t2. **Grade the reader’s chart.** In `grade.mjs`, `table.mjs` and `test/grade.browser.test.mjs`, check the caption and semantic headers for every series, correct the signup reference label, and accept equivalent unambiguous date formats. Check plotted order and breaks at the four known missing dates, sample a reader date in both zones, and check clipping when a title is visible. Add controls for a bridged categorical line, agent-filtered revenue and a genuine omission whose note must have the right count in the accessibility tree and on screen. The repository already has a painted-gap check to reuse. [Existing F2 test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f2-gaps.browser.test.ts:1)\n    29\t3. **Freeze the release rule in `README.md`, `run.mjs` and `summary.mjs`.** My proposed minimum is three fresh, uncoached attempts per task with Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code Opus 5.5, pinned to observed model versions. That gives 24 attempts. Allow documented self-checks within each 20-minute session, with no grader feedback. Require 3/3 in every model–task cell for a 1.0 rehearsal pass. Keep failures and timeouts. Record and replace only documented voids or infrastructure failures. Save CLI versions, effective effort, input and grader hashes, and per-cell counts. Three successes give a two-sided exact 95% interval of roughly 29–100% per cell under independent runs. Report the observed counts as release rehearsal evidence limited to these prompts, models, line/bar charts and Chrome environments. They establish neither a general pass rate nor human usability.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Would be better.** Shuffle the quarters in `regional-sales.json` and derive chronological quarter order independently in `fixtures.test.mjs`. Categories retain encounter order, so this would add an order mistake an agent can actually make. A second grading input with changed values would also distinguish a working `render(element, data)` from a hard-coded answer.\n    32\t\n    33\tThis was a read-only review. I ran the independent fixture comparisons and comparator/audit probes. The browser test count comes from the coordinator. No contract change is needed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
