date } from 'axylo'; import { chartOptions } from './chart.js'; const rows = JSON.parse(readFileSync('data.json', 'utf8')); const problems = validate('bar', chartOptions(rows)); console.log('dev build diagnostics:', problems.length, problems.map(d => d.code).join(',')); const d = validate('bar', { ...chartOptio\n144 TEXT I've written `chart.js`. Its default export `render(element, data)` draws a grouped bar chart titled \"Quarterly sales by region\" into the element you pass and returns the Axylo chart handle. There's one group per quarter, with a North, Central and West bar in each. I checked it with `validate()` in Node only. I didn't draw it in a browser\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/pages/data.md | head -235 && nl -ba apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md | head -175 && rg -n -i 'rehearsal|DX12' apps/docs/pages/{conformance,stability}.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/pages/data.md | head -235 && nl -ba apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md | head -175 && rg -n -i 'rehearsal|DX12' apps/docs/pages/{conformance,stability}.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Your data\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n     4\t\n     5\t## One table, three shapes\n     6\t\n     7\tTake four days with two readings per day: sessions for two channels.\n     8\t\n     9\t| day | Direct | Referral |\n    10\t|---|---|---|\n    11\t| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n    12\t| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n    13\t| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n    14\t| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n    15\t\n    16\tAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n    17\t\n    18\t### Long rows\n    19\t\n    20\tOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field whose value says which series a row belongs to; that value is the series' key and its name.\n    21\t\n    22\t[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n    23\t\n    24\t### Wide rows\n    25\t\n    26\tOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). Each field is a series: its key is the field name (`direct`), its name for the reader is the field's `label` (`'Direct'`). Use the key in `colors` and in event handlers, and the label for display. The series share one value axis: the first field's `domain`, `scale` and `tickFormat` set it (a `tickFormat` on a later field is reported, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)), and with more than one field the axis is titled \"Value\" (the `value` message). The chart's generated name and its summary name the series rather than that axis (\"Line chart of Visits and Signups by day\", with fields labelled `'Visits'` and `'Signups'`). When the series have different units, the summary gives each series' range in its own unit and no range for the chart as a whole.\n    27\t\n    28\t[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n    29\t\n    30\t### Typed columns\n    31\t\n    32\t`data: { columns }` with one array-like per field (an array or a typed array), all of the same length; different lengths are error [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing. This shape suits large data, because Axylo can read such a column in place; see [what Axylo reads, keeps and copies](data.md#what-axylo-reads-keeps-and-copies).\n    33\t\n    34\t[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n    35\t\n    36\t## Fields\n    37\t\n    38\tA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n    39\t\n    40\t```ts\n    41\tx: 'day'\n    42\ty: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'visits' }\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\tThe object form adds a label (the axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements), a unit, formats, a scale, a missing-value rule and author bounds. The [options reference](options.md#value-fields) lists them all.\n    46\t\n    47\tWith TypeScript, field names are checked against your row type. A misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, or wide `y` together with `series` does not compile. With untyped rows (from `fetch().json()`, say) the same mistakes are reported at run time: a field that no row has is [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), and the message lists the keys the rows do have and, when one is close, the closest match. With no rows, there is nothing to check a field against, and nothing is reported.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Three kinds of time\n    50\t\n    51\tDates are where charts most often go wrong without anyone noticing. Axylo keeps three kinds apart and never mixes them silently.\n    52\t\n    53\t| Kind | Looks like | Means | Shown |\n    54\t|---|---|---|---|\n    55\t| Calendar date | `'2026-02-24'`, `'2026-02'`, `'2026'` | A day, month or year, wherever you are | The same in every time zone |\n    56\t| Instant | Epoch milliseconds with `type: 'time'`, a `Date`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00Z'`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00+01:00'` | One moment, the same everywhere | In `timeZone` (default: the viewer's zone), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the summary |\n    57\t| Wall-clock time | `'2026-02-24T09:30'` (no offset) | What a clock showed, zone unknown | As written, with hint [AX112](diagnostics.md#ax112) |\n    58\t\n    59\t### The off-by-one-day trap\n    60\t\n    61\tA daily metric is a calendar date. Many back ends send it as an instant at UTC midnight: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'`, or the epoch milliseconds of that moment. For a viewer in New York that moment is the evening of 23 February, so every daily value appears one day early. In Berlin it is still the 24th. Showing the instant in the viewer's zone is correct for an instant; the data said \"this moment\", not \"this day\".\n    62\t\n    63\tThe example below has the same five values sent both ways. Your browser's time zone decides how the first chart reads: west of UTC, its first point is on the 23rd.\n    64\t\n    65\t[example: dates](../examples/dates.ts)\n    66\t\n    67\tAxylo notices at least two distinct instants that all fall at the same time of day, whole days apart, and raises hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). The fix is one of:\n    68\t\n    69\t- Send calendar dates: `'2026-02-24'`. `new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10)` turns a UTC midnight into one.\n    70\t- Keep the values and declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Axylo then reads each instant (string, number or `Date`) as the calendar day it falls on in UTC, as the [daily example](getting-started.md#daily-data-five-things-to-get-right) does.\n    71\t- If they really are instants and everyone should see UTC, set `x: { field: 'day', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' }`.\n    72\t\n    73\t### How Axylo decides\n    74\t\n    75\tWhen `type` is not given, each value of the x field votes for a kind:\n    76\t\n    77\t- `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY` strings for a calendar date;\n    78\t- date-time strings with `Z` or an offset, and `Date` objects, for an instant;\n    79\t- date-time strings without an offset for a wall-clock time;\n    80\t- finite numbers for a number. A bare number is never taken for an instant: epoch milliseconds need `type: 'time'`, and numbers that look like timestamps raise hint [AX109](diagnostics.md#ax109);\n    81\t- anything else for a category.\n    82\t\n    83\tCategories win when they are at least as many as all the other votes together. Otherwise the kind with the most votes wins, ties going to date, then instant, then wall-clock time, then number. Values of any other kind are then unusable: they are not drawn, and they raise [AX107](diagnostics.md#ax107) with a note to the reader. Three category strings, two dates and two numbers therefore make a date axis that keeps the two dates. When a feed mixes kinds, declare `type`.\n    84\t\n    85\tCategories keep the order in which they first appear.\n    86\t\n    87\t`timeZone` takes `'local'` (the default, the viewer's zone), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone such as `'Europe/Paris'`. It applies to instants. A zone the browser does not know is [AX152](diagnostics.md#ax152), and the viewer's zone is used.\n    88\t\n    89\tCalendar periods other than years and months (quarters, ISO weeks) are categories in 1.0: write them as you want them read (`'2025 Q1'`) and set `ordered: true` on the x field so that their labels may be thinned when they do not fit.\n    90\t\n    91\t## Missing values\n    92\t\n    93\tAxylo never draws a missing value as zero unless you ask, and does not connect a line across a missing value it knows about. It knows about one when your data says so:\n    94\t\n    95\t| In your data | Drawn | Reported |\n    96\t|---|---|---|\n    97\t| `null`, or `''` | A gap | Nothing: this is how to say \"no data\" |\n    98\t| `NaN` in a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array` | A gap | Nothing |\n    99\t| The value field absent from a row (`undefined`) | A gap | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n   100\t| The x or series field absent from a row | The row is not drawn | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n   101\t| `NaN` or `Infinity` in rows | A gap | Warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103), and the reader's note |\n   102\t| `0` | Zero | Nothing |\n   103\t\n   104\tA day with **no row at all** is different: the data says nothing about it. Axylo infers that a step is missing only from a series' own rows, when the x axis is dates, instants, wall-clock times or numbers and the series is regular: it has at least three rows, every interval between neighbouring rows is a whole multiple of the smallest one, and at least half of the intervals are the smallest one. A missing step it infers is drawn as a gap, as if the row held `null`, with hint [AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105). It infers nothing before the first row or after the last, in a series of one or two rows, in an irregular series, or when there would be more than twice as many missing steps as rows (and more than 1,000). Another series having a row at that x does not make the step missing in this one: a slot a series simply has no row for (a weekly series beside a daily one) is not a gap, its line is joined across it, and a bar chart draws no bar and no \"no data\" mark there. In a stack it is different, see below. So **when you know a day is missing, send it with `null`**: that is the instruction Axylo always follows.\n   105\t\n   106\tA value standing alone between two gaps is drawn as a point, so it does not vanish. On a bar chart a missing value gets a small \"no data\" mark (at the baseline, or for a stacked bar at the edge of its stack), distinct from a zero bar. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". When a warning means values were left out, the chart shows a short note to the reader (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\") inside the figure, in every build.\n   107\t\n   108\t**In a stack** (stacked areas or bars), a series with no value at an x is a missing part, whatever the reason: a `null`, an inferred missing step, or no row there at all. The part has zero height, the series' line follows its band, and the total at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, so a stack never shows a total made of values the data does not contain. A weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; see [the area chart's example](area.md#edge-case-a-missing-part-of-a-stack).\n   109\t\n   110\tFor counts, where a day without a row means nothing happened, declare it: `missing: 'zero'` turns null, absent and inferred-missing values of that field into zeros, and the note says the values are shown differently from the data.\n   111\t\n   112\t[example: missing](../examples/missing.ts)\n   113\t\n   114\tHere are the values an application sends by mistake, all at once. The note under the chart is what the reader sees; the console has one line per code.\n   115\t\n   116\t[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n   117\t\n   118\t## Numbers sent as strings\n   119\t\n   120\tSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo looks at the whole field:\n   121\t\n   122\t- When every value is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`, and exponent notation such as `'1e3'`), the field is converted and drawn, and hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to say so with `parse: 'number'`. With that, there is no hint, and TypeScript accepts the string field on a value axis.\n   123\t- When the field also holds a number, or any string that is not a plain decimal (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`), or another kind of value (`true`), every string in it is rejected, plain decimals included. They are not drawn, and they are warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n   124\t\n   125\tThe conversion is to JavaScript numbers. Integers are exact up to 9,007,199,254,740,991 (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`); a larger one is rounded to the nearest number JavaScript can hold, without a diagnostic: `'9007199254740993'` reads 9,007,199,254,740,992. A string too large for a number (`'1e999'`) becomes infinite and is [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103). When exact counts could exceed that limit, check them with `Number.isSafeInteger(Number(value))` before charting.\n   126\t\n   127\t[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n   128\t\n   129\t## Order and duplicates\n   130\t\n   131\tRows can come in any order. Line, area and scatter charts are drawn in x order, and each value stays with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n   132\t\n   133\t[example: unsorted](../examples/unsorted.ts)\n   134\t\n   135\tOn a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows with the same x in the same series are error [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): that series is not drawn, while the other series are; with no series left to draw, the chart shows its error state and keeps its table. Axylo does not know whether you meant the sum, the last or the average. Two donut slices with the same label are the same error, and the donut is not drawn. On a scatter chart repeated x values, and repeated points, are ordinary data.\n   136\t\n   137\t## Units, formats and percentages\n   138\t\n   139\t| Option on a value field | What it does |\n   140\t|---|---|\n   141\t| `label` | The axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: the field name. |\n   142\t| `unit` | Follows every value in tooltip, table and announcements, and is part of the axis title. |\n   143\t| `format` | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements: `Intl.NumberFormat` options, or a function. Default: the locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits (`123,456.75`). |\n   144\t| `tickFormat` | How axis ticks are written. Default: the locale's grouping with the fraction digits the tick step needs (`50,000`); compact (`1.2M`) when the largest tick is at least a million; scientific from 10¹⁵. |\n   145\t| `scale: 'percent'` | The values are percentages from 0 to 100. |\n   146\t| `scale: 'ratio'` | The values are fractions from 0 to 1, shown as percentages. |\n   147\t\n   148\tA percent or ratio scale is declared, never guessed from a label. Its axis ends at full scale (100%) only when the largest value is at least half of full scale and not above it; otherwise it ends at the first tick at or above the data, so 4% is not drawn as a sliver of 100%. Numbers and dates are formatted for the `locale` option if given, else the document's language (`<html lang>`), else the browser's. A formatter that fails (a function that throws, or `Intl` options that `Intl` rejects) is warning [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160), and the default format is used for that value.\n   149\t\n   150\t[example: units](../examples/units.ts)\n   151\t\n   152\tBar and area value axes always include zero, since a bar's length and an area's height are their values. A `domain` that leaves zero out is widened, with warning [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). Line and scatter axes fit the data.\n   153\t\n   154\tValue axes (y, and both axes of a scatter chart) end on a drawn tick at or beyond the data, the reference lines and bands, and stack totals; in a plot too narrow to label an end tick, the axis takes the next coarser step. A date, time or number x axis of a line, area or bar chart spans exactly its data instead (for bars, plus half a band at each end), so a time series is not padded to the next round tick.\n   155\t\n   156\t## What Axylo reads, keeps and copies\n   157\t\n   158\t- **Rows** are read when the chart is created or updated, and never changed. Their values are copied into Axylo's own typed arrays. Events hand back your own row object (`point.row`), and diagnostics name rows by their index in your data. A position no row of yours holds (an inferred missing day, a donut's \"Other\") has `row: null` and `index: -1`; check before you look a row up.\n   159\t- **Typed columns** of values (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`) are read in place, without a copy, when Axylo can: the x values are already in order, no missing step is inferred, and no value has to change (no `Infinity`, no `missing: 'zero'` to apply). Otherwise Axylo copies what it needs; grouping long columns into series and scatter charts can need new storage too. Either way, do not change an array after passing it: to change the data, pass new arrays with `update({ data })`.\n   160\t- **Appended rows** are read once and added to the chart's own storage; `keep` limits how much is kept. See [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md#append-and-keep).\n   161\t\n   162\t## Checking data before you draw\n   163\t\n   164\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as a chart, without a page, and returns the diagnostics. Use it in Node, in a unit test, or in a coding agent's loop before rendering. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n     1\t# Getting started\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can install Axylo, draw a first chart from rows with npm or a script tag, use it inside a framework, get the five things right that most often make a daily chart wrong, and know what to check when a chart looks wrong.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Install\n     6\t\n     7\tWith npm and a bundler:\n     8\t\n     9\t```sh\n    10\tnpm install axylo\n    11\t```\n    12\t\n    13\tWith a script tag, load one file from the package's `dist` folder: `axylo.iife.js` is the development build (it prints hints), `axylo.iife.min.js` the production build. Both define one global, `Axylo`.\n    14\t\n    15\tThe package has no runtime dependencies. Its module build comes in two flavours, chosen by the `development` export condition: a bundler that resolves that condition gets the development build, which also prints hints; otherwise you get the production build, which prints errors and warnings only. The styles need no import: the library adopts its stylesheet as a constructed style sheet, or, where that is not possible, adds a `<style>` element. A page that prefers to load the stylesheet itself can link `axylo/axylo.css`, and the library then uses that copy. The packed packages are tested in Chrome under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline`, with the stylesheet adopted and with it linked (`tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts`).\n    16\t\n    17\tTypeScript 5.4 or later gives you checked field names. The package's declarations refer to DOM types (`HTMLElement`), so a TypeScript project needs the `DOM` library in `lib`, also for code that only calls `validate()` in Node. Plain JavaScript works the same without types.\n    18\t\n    19\t## The first chart\n    20\t\n    21\tEach of these runs live below its source. They are kept in the repository as the files the package's distribution tests install and render (`examples/first-chart`), and each says what page it needs around it.\n    22\t\n    23\tWith npm and a bundler:\n    24\t\n    25\t[example: first-chart-npm](../../../examples/first-chart/npm.ts)\n    26\t\n    27\tWith a script tag:\n    28\t\n    29\t[example: first-chart-script](../../../examples/first-chart/script.html)\n    30\t\n    31\tPoint at a chart, or press Tab to reach its plot and use the arrow keys, or open its table. The only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but without it (or `labelledBy`) the chart gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n    32\t\n    33\t## Using Axylo in a framework\n    34\t\n    35\tNo framework adapter ships in 1.0; adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others are planned after 1.0. Axylo is called from any framework's own hooks: create the chart when its element exists, call `update()` when your data or options change, and call `destroy()` when the element goes away. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element is supported; it is the sequence a framework's development mode produces.\n    36\t\n    37\tThe lifecycle as three plain functions:\n    38\t\n    39\t[example: lifecycle](../examples/lifecycle.ts)\n    40\t\n    41\t## Daily data: five things to get right\n    42\t\n    43\tThe first chart above has months as calendar dates and numbers as numbers. Data from an API is often less tidy. Here is a daily metric as many back ends send it, and the chart that reads it correctly:\n    44\t\n    45\t[example: daily-sessions](../examples/daily-sessions.ts)\n    46\t\n    47\t1. **Days sent as UTC midnights.** For daily totals sent as `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` (or as epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight), use `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Without it they are instants, shown in the viewer's time zone, so west of UTC each value appears on the day before, with only hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). Strings such as `'2026-02-24'` need no `type`. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).\n    48\t2. **Numbers sent as strings.** For decimal strings, use `y: { field: 'sessions', parse: 'number' }`. They are converted to JavaScript numbers, which are exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991; a larger count is rounded without a warning, so check exact counts with `Number.isSafeInteger` first. A field that mixes numbers and strings is not converted at all. See [numbers sent as strings](data.md#numbers-sent-as-strings).\n    49\t3. **Row order.** Pass rows in any order. Axylo sorts dated rows by x and keeps each value with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n    50\t4. **A day with no data.** When you know a day is missing, send it, with `sessions: null`. Axylo can infer a missing day only between two rows of a series that has at least three rows at a regular step, and never before the first row or after the last; with two rows, or an irregular series, the line runs straight across the missing day. See [missing values](data.md#missing-values).\n    51\t5. **A misspelled field.** On error [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), compare the field you named with the keys the message lists, which come from your rows, and use the suggested spelling when one is offered. With no rows at all the keys cannot be checked, so the error appears once data arrives. With typed rows in TypeScript the typo does not compile.\n    52\t\n    53\t## What you get without asking\n    54\t\n    55\t| Feature | What it does |\n    56\t|---|---|\n    57\t| Name and summary | `title` is the visible heading and the chart's accessible name. In a card that already has a heading, `labelledBy` names the chart with it. The chart's description is a generated summary: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. `description` adds to it. |\n    58\t| Table view | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table, 100 rows per page; above 100 rows a pager and a \"Jump to\" field appear. |\n    59\t| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, where the arrow keys move through the points and its value text describes the point in focus, and the toolbar, when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. See [the keyboard map](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map). |\n    60\t| Reading values | Pointing in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule (nearest x for line and area, the band for bars, the nearest point within 24 px for scatter, the slice for a donut). The tooltip sits against the marks it describes, not at the pointer, and lets the pointer through; rest on a point to move onto its tooltip, click to pin it. In a small plot the same text goes in a strip under the plot. Escape closes it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n    61\t| Colour and more than colour | Eight default colours that work in light and dark pages. Lines and areas also carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars take fill patterns past the eighth series. |\n    62\t| Page theme | Font, text colour and light or dark come from the page. The chart repaints when the page switches scheme. |\n    63\t| Size | The chart fills its container's width. With no height set, the plot is 16:9 of the width, between 160 and 480 px tall. It follows the container as it resizes, with no call from you. |\n    64\t| States | No rows shows \"No data to show.\" inside the plot, never an empty frame or an invented axis. `state` sets loading, empty and error messages of your own. |\n    65\t| Diagnostics | Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported with a code, the field and rows where they apply, and the fix. When values were left out or changed, the reader is told too. |\n    66\t\n    67\tThe four states, each a chart with no rows:\n    68\t\n    69\t[example: states](../examples/states.ts)\n    70\t\n    71\t## When it looks wrong: three things to check\n    72\t\n    73\t**1. Read the diagnostics.** Open the browser console. Every problem Axylo found is printed once per code per page as `[axylo AX…] \"Chart title\": what is wrong, where, and how to fix it`. The same list is on the chart as `chart.diagnostics`, current as soon as the call returns. Each code has a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). Hints, which are advice rather than problems, print in the development build only.\n    74\t\n    75\t**2. Check which kind of x Axylo read.** A date off by one day almost always means instants where calendar dates were meant (item 1 above). If the tooltip heading names a time zone, Axylo read instants. If the x labels are your strings in input order, it read categories.\n    76\t\n    77\t**3. Check the values without drawing.** `validate()` runs the same checks as creating the chart, in Node or in a test, with the same options as the chart:\n    78\t\n    79\t```ts\n    80\timport { validate } from 'axylo';\n    81\t\n    82\tconst problems = validate('line', { title: 'Monthly visitors', data: rows, x: 'month', y: 'visitors' });\n    83\tfor (const d of problems) console.log(d.code, d.level, d.message);\n    84\t```\n    85\t\n    86\tNumbers that arrive as strings, a missing day, two rows for the same day and a misspelled field each have their own code; [Your data](data.md) covers each case.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Next\n    89\t\n    90\tRead [Your data](data.md) next: it explains the three shapes rows can take, how dates are read, and what happens to missing values.\napps/docs/pages/stability.md:23:**What it does not cover.** The chart's internal DOM structure and class names, the custom properties listed as internal, the roles the plot uses to talk to assistive technology (chosen without screen-reader rehearsal, and changeable in a minor release if rehearsal shows a better pattern), and the exact pixels of the drawing.\napps/docs/pages/stability.md:39:Tested so far, on Linux: the library's acceptance tests in Chrome and in Playwright's builds of Firefox and WebKit (Playwright's WebKit, not Safari), and the distribution tests in Chrome (they install the packed package, also under a Content Security Policy). Safari itself, Edge, Windows, macOS and phones have not been tested. No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0. What the acceptance checker recorded for this revision is on the [conformance page](conformance.md); see also [Accessibility](accessibility.md#what-has-and-has-not-been-verified).\napps/docs/pages/conformance.md:10:- **No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0.** No screen reader has been run against these charts, so nothing in this documentation says what a screen reader speaks.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md | head -150 && rg -n -i 'parse|row getter|unit|messages.value|self-check|validate\\\\(|ordered|value axis' apps/docs/pages/{options,bar,line}.md && rg -n 'DECIMAL|decimalPattern|decimal.*\\\\\"'^|PLAIN_DECIMAL'\"' packages/axylo/src\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md | head -150 && rg -n -i 'parse|row getter|unit|messages.value|self-check|validate\\\\(|ordered|value axis' apps/docs/pages/{options,bar,line}.md && rg -n 'DECIMAL|decimalPattern|decimal.*\\\\\"'^|PLAIN_DECIMAL'\"' packages/axylo/src\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Diagnostics\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can read an Axylo diagnostic, collect diagnostics in your own code, make a chart throw on them, and check a chart's data in Node or in a test before anything is drawn. Every code has its own page, listed at the end.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Reading one\n     6\t\n     7\tWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n     8\t\n     9\t[output: AX103](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n    10\t\n    11\t| Part | Here |\n    12\t|---|---|\n    13\t| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n    14\t| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n    15\t| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. A diagnostic about the chart as a whole (no name, too many series, low contrast) has no field and no rows. |\n    16\t| Fix | What to change. |\n    17\t| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n    18\t\n    19\t## Levels and who is told\n    20\t\n    21\t| Level | Means | Console |\n    22\t|---|---|---|\n    23\t| Error | Something cannot be drawn as given: the chart, a series, or the donut. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n    24\t| Warning | Something is not drawn as given, or an option is ignored. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n    25\t| Hint | Advice for you. | Development builds only, with `console.info` |\n    26\t\n    27\tThe level says who is told; it does not by itself say what stays on screen. That depends on the code, and each code's page says it, with a checked minimal input. For example: an error about one series ([AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108)) leaves the other series drawn; a warning can put the chart in its error state when the ignored option was needed to draw at all (a `y` list on a scatter chart, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)); and some hints come with a visible change: an inferred missing step is drawn as a gap ([AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105)), a plot too small to draw is replaced by its summary ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)).\n    28\t\n    29\tWhen values were left out or changed, the chart shows the reader a note in the figure (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\", or \"Values shown differently from the data\"), in every build, and in the accessibility tree.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Collecting them in your code\n    32\t\n    33\tEach diagnostic is an object:\n    34\t\n    35\t```ts\n    36\tinterface Diagnostic {\n    37\t  code: `AX${number}`;           // 'AX103'\n    38\t  level: 'error' | 'warning' | 'hint';\n    39\t  message: string;               // what is wrong, where, and how to fix it\n    40\t  chart: string;                 // the chart's title, else its element id\n    41\t  field?: string;                // when it is about a field\n    42\t  rows?: readonly number[];      // first rows, in your data, at most 10\n    43\t  count?: number;                // how many values or rows in all\n    44\t}\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\t`chart.diagnostics` lists them for the current options and data, up to date as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. `onDiagnostic` is called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build and at every level, so it is the place to send them to your error tracking. A listener that throws is reported once and otherwise ignored; it cannot break the chart.\n    48\t\n    49\t[example: diagnostics-listener](../examples/diagnostics-listener.html)\n    50\t\n    51\t## When they are found\n    52\t\n    53\tMost diagnostics are found while a call checks its input: the create, `update` or `append` call. Those are in `chart.diagnostics` when the call returns. A few can only be found later, while drawing: low contrast after the page's CSS changes ([AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141)), a plot too small ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), labels that stop fitting after a resize ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)), more bars than pixels ([AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132)), a formatter that fails ([AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160)), a `labelledBy` that names no element when there is no `title` ([AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140)), and an internal failure ([AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)). They are reported when found, through the same console line and `onDiagnostic`. A `zoom()` call that cannot apply (a category axis, a donut, a compact chart) is reported when it is made, through the console line and `onDiagnostic` only: it changes nothing, so `chart.diagnostics` does not list it ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)).\n    54\t\n    55\t## Strict mode\n    56\t\n    57\t`strict: true` makes the chart throw on any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. The chart keeps its last valid state; a rejected create leaves nothing in the page. The error is named `AxyloStrictError`, and its `diagnostics` property has every diagnostic of the rejected call. Hints never throw, and neither do diagnostics found later while drawing. A failure while a call is being checked (such as a row getter that throws, [AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)) rejects that call too, with AX199 after what it had found; a failure later, while drawing, is never thrown. Each code's page says whether a strict chart throws it. See the example in [Updating](updating.md#strict-mode).\n    58\t\n    59\t## validate() in Node and in tests\n    60\t\n    61\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as creating a chart (options, data, view, name) without a DOM, and returns the diagnostics a created chart would report from its call. It takes the same options as the chart type, so it type-checks the same way. Importing `axylo` in Node does not touch the DOM.\n    62\t\n    63\tUse it on a server, in a build step, or in a coding agent's loop:\n    64\t\n    65\t[source: examples/node/validate.ts](../examples/node/validate.ts)\n    66\t\n    67\tOr in a unit test, so that a change in the data a query returns fails the build instead of a dashboard:\n    68\t\n    69\t[source: examples/node/chart-data.test.ts](../examples/node/chart-data.test.ts)\n    70\t\n    71\tBoth files are type-checked and run by this documentation's own tests, against the packed package. The package's declarations refer to DOM types, so a TypeScript configuration for Node code needs `\"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\"]` (or `skipLibCheck`). `validate()` cannot find what only a page can show: the diagnostics listed under \"When they are found\" need a browser. It can tell that a chart has neither `title` nor `labelledBy`, but not whether a `labelledBy` id exists.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Every code\n    74\t\n    75\t[table: diagnostic codes](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\napps/docs/pages/line.md:7:A line shows how a measure changed along an ordered x: sessions per day, latency per minute, revenue per month. Several series compare trends. The x can be calendar dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories.\napps/docs/pages/line.md:42:| Markers | Each series carries its marker shape at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip, so a dense line still has some. Every point gets a marker only when neighbouring points are at least 12 px apart. A point alone between gaps always has one. |\napps/docs/pages/line.md:48:The value axis of a line fits the data and need not start at zero, unlike a bar or area.\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:7:Bars compare categories: satisfaction by team, tickets by product area, orders per region. A bar's length is its value, so the value axis always includes zero. Bars over a date axis compare counts per period (orders per day).\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:22:| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:40:| Baseline | The value axis always includes zero; an author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). Grouped bars grow from zero. |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:43:| Long labels | Remedies in order: thinning (date, number and `ordered` category axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, truncating in the middle with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, the chart says \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" and [AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143) suggests `horizontal: true`. Axylo never switches orientation by itself: that would change what the chart says at a glance. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:63:| `y` | `ValueField<Row> \\| readonly ValueField<Row>[]` | required | The value field, or one field per series. See [Value fields](#value-fields). The first field sets the shared value axis's domain and scale. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:116:| `x` | `ScatterXField<Row>` | required | The x field: like an [x field](#x-fields), with `type` limited to `'number'`, `'time'` or `'date'`, and no `ordered`. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:130:A value field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers (or `null`), or an object. A key whose values are strings is accepted only in the object form with `parse: 'number'`.\napps/docs/pages/options.md:135:| `field` | `NumberKey<Row> \\| StringKey<Row>` | required | The key of the row: a number field, or a string field with `parse`. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:136:| `parse` | `'number'` | none | The field holds numbers as strings; convert them to JavaScript numbers (exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991). |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:138:| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows values in tooltip, table and announcements: `'ms'`, `'USD'`. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:154:| `ordered` | `boolean` | `false` | Category axes: the categories have a natural order, so labels may be thinned when they do not fit. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:157:| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows x values in tooltip, table and announcements. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:162:For scatter charts the x field takes the same keys except `ordered`, and `type` without `'category'`:\napps/docs/pages/options.md:171:| `unit` | `string` | none | As above. |\napps/docs/pages/options.md:230:`validate(type, options)` takes `'line'`, `'area'`, `'bar'`, `'scatter'` or `'donut'` and that type's options, runs the checks a created chart runs from its call without a DOM, and returns `Diagnostic[]`. It cannot find what needs a page: contrast, sizes, label fitting, formatters, or whether a `labelledBy` id exists. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\npackages/axylo/src/data/values.ts:6:const decimal = /^[+-]?\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?(?:[eE][+-]?\\d+)?$/;\npackages/axylo/src/format/number.ts:42:/** A finite non-zero double as the shortest decimal that reads back as it: d.ddd × 10^exponent. */\npackages/axylo/src/format/number.ts:57:/** The decimal × 10^shift as a string Intl formats exactly. */\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
