{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\| F[136] |\"'^'\"\\\\| D[12] |\"'^'\"\\\\| DX12' docs/02-product-spec.md && nl -ba rehearsal/README.md | sed -n '19,32p'\"","aggregated_output":"95:| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n97:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n100:| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n234:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n    19\t## Isolation and audit\n    20\t\n    21\tEach attempt gets a newly created directory outside the repository with a fresh home, temporary storage and workspace. The workspace contains copies of the published docs, the unpacked package, primary data and prompt, and a writable location for `chart.js` and its helpers. The docs retain published examples and exclude internal source snapshots. The runner applies `chmod -R a-w` to `docs/` and `node_modules/` and removes write permission from the fixture, prompt and package manifest. These file modes protect against accidental writes. A sufficiently privileged or same-owner subprocess can still change modes or replace inputs. The runner verifies staged input hashes before grading, and the browser grader supplies canonical data from the harness.\n    22\t\n    23\t- **Codex CLI 0.170.0.** The runner assigns a fresh `CODEX_HOME` and symlinks only `auth.json` from the real login. It uses `--ignore-user-config` and `--ignore-rules`, disables apps, plugins, memory and hooks, skips host skill discovery, and disables web search. It uses `-s workspace-write`, `-a never`, and `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=false`. The CLI help describes `--sandbox` as selecting the sandbox for “model-generated shell commands” and says `--ignore-user-config` still uses `CODEX_HOME` for auth. Workspace-write applies write and network restrictions to agent commands. It allows host reads, and Codex's shell can run more than Node. The prompt and audit constrain use of that wider surface.\n    24\t- **Claude Code 2.1.327.** Each attempt has a fresh `HOME` and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. With an API key, the runner passes it through and uses `--bare`, which skips OAuth, auto-memory and `CLAUDE.md` discovery. For OAuth the only initial config entry is a `.credentials.json` symlink into the real home. The runner creates and removes the symlink without opening the credential file. The fresh config and staged workspace contain no local Claude customizations or saved memory. Without `--bare`, `--safe-mode` disables `CLAUDE.md` discovery, skills, installed plugins, hooks and other customizations, and `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` turns off auto-memory. Built-in plugins, admin-managed settings and managed plugin hooks can still apply.\n    25\t- Both Claude modes keep `--restricted`, which ignores user, project and local settings and confines file tools to working directories. `--strict-mcp-config` limits MCP to the supplied empty configuration, `--disable-slash-commands` disables skills and commands, and `--no-session-persistence` turns off session saves in print mode. The built-in allowlist exposes file tools and Bash, with `--allowedTools` limiting Bash permission to `node …` and `dontAsk` denying other calls. `--permission-prompts none` denies anything that would prompt. Web tools and Chrome are absent. A Node subprocess can have host filesystem and network access through the operating system.\n    26\t- Both CLIs need their provider transport. These CLI controls give fresh session state and restrict configured tools. They do not provide a filesystem namespace or a transport-only network policy for Claude's Node subprocess. For strictly enforced docs-only reads and network isolation, use external isolation that enforces those boundaries for both CLIs. Keep host managed policies fixed and record them with the rehearsal if they apply.\n    27\t\n    28\tThe audit requires a parseable transcript with the CLI's start and terminal events. Empty, malformed, or truncated transcripts are **unverifiable**. The audit takes paths from file-tool inputs and literal shell file operands, including redirects and paths relative to an explicit working directory or a `cd` command. It skips command bodies blocked by the CLI before execution, file contents, inline programs, search patterns, tool output and assistant prose. The audit can resolve symlinks when it has filesystem evidence. “No findings” describes the transcript only. A subprocess's actual reads, dynamically constructed paths and fetches from Node code are outside the transcript's coverage.\n    29\t\n    30\tThe attempt's workspace, fresh home and config, and temporary storage are all inside its run directory. Recorded accesses to the repository, the real home, another attempt or the linked login void an attempt, even for missing files and after a timeout. System tools are allowed. Other outside paths are recorded under “looked outside the workspace,” with each path's existence. Only existing paths in that group void the attempt. Excluded tools also make a completed audit ineligible for grading.\n    31\t\n    32\tEach record stores `run.json` with the CLI version, arguments, requested and observed model metadata, elapsed time and status, and SHA-256 hashes of both inputs, their expected results, the prompt, copied docs, packed library and grader. Claude records include `claudeMode` as `bare` or `fresh-config`. The record also stores the transcript, stderr, answer files if present, and the grade and Chrome version if the answer reached Chrome. Audit version 2 records the original workspace and the filesystem observations used to resolve paths. The disposable workspace and home, including the credential symlink, are removed after saving the record. An interrupted batch may leave an incomplete record or scratch directory.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
