{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md | sed -n '7,28p;55,72p' && nl -ba apps/docs/pages/bar.md | sed -n '13,32p' && rg -n 'AX10[146]|AX111' apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs | head -14 && nl -ba rehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs | sed -n '1,210p' | rg 'it\\\\(|test\\\\(|wrong|negative|reject|omission|primary'\"","aggregated_output":"     7\tWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n     8\t\n     9\t[output: AX103](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n    10\t\n    11\t| Part | Here |\n    12\t|---|---|\n    13\t| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n    14\t| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n    15\t| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. A diagnostic about the chart as a whole (no name, too many series, low contrast) has no field and no rows. |\n    16\t| Fix | What to change. |\n    17\t| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n    18\t\n    19\t## Levels and who is told\n    20\t\n    21\t| Level | Means | Console |\n    22\t|---|---|---|\n    23\t| Error | Something cannot be drawn as given: the chart, a series, or the donut. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n    24\t| Warning | Something is not drawn as given, or an option is ignored. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n    25\t| Hint | Advice for you. | Development builds only, with `console.info` |\n    26\t\n    27\tThe level says who is told; it does not by itself say what stays on screen. That depends on the code, and each code's page says it, with a checked minimal input. For example: an error about one series ([AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108)) leaves the other series drawn; a warning can put the chart in its error state when the ignored option was needed to draw at all (a `y` list on a scatter chart, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)); and some hints come with a visible change: an inferred missing step is drawn as a gap ([AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105)), a plot too small to draw is replaced by its summary ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)).\n    28\t\n    55\t## Strict mode\n    56\t\n    57\t`strict: true` makes the chart throw on any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. The chart keeps its last valid state; a rejected create leaves nothing in the page. The error is named `AxyloStrictError`, and its `diagnostics` property has every diagnostic of the rejected call. Hints never throw, and neither do diagnostics found later while drawing. A failure while a call is being checked (such as a row getter that throws, [AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)) rejects that call too, with AX199 after what it had found; a failure later, while drawing, is never thrown. Each code's page says whether a strict chart throws it. See the example in [Updating](updating.md#strict-mode).\n    58\t\n    59\t## validate() in Node and in tests\n    60\t\n    61\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as creating a chart (options, data, view, name) without a DOM, and returns the diagnostics a created chart would report from its call. It takes the same options as the chart type, so it type-checks the same way. Importing `axylo` in Node does not touch the DOM.\n    62\t\n    63\tUse it on a server, in a build step, or in a coding agent's loop:\n    64\t\n    65\t[source: examples/node/validate.ts](../examples/node/validate.ts)\n    66\t\n    67\tOr in a unit test, so that a change in the data a query returns fails the build instead of a dashboard:\n    68\t\n    69\t[source: examples/node/chart-data.test.ts](../examples/node/chart-data.test.ts)\n    70\t\n    71\tBoth files are type-checked and run by this documentation's own tests, against the packed package. The package's declarations refer to DOM types, so a TypeScript configuration for Node code needs `\"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\"]` (or `skipLibCheck`). `validate()` cannot find what only a page can show: the diagnostics listed under \"When they are found\" need a browser. It can tell that a chart has neither `title` nor `labelledBy`, but not whether a `labelledBy` id exists.\n    72\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { bar } from 'axylo';\n    17\tbar(target, { data, x, y, series, layout: 'grouped', horizontal: false, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On a bar chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\n    23\t| `y`, `series` | One value field (with `series` for long rows), or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\n    24\t| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\n    25\t| `horizontal` | `true` draws the bars from the left, with the categories down the side. Default: `false`. A `y` reference then runs up and down the plot, and an `x` reference across it. |\n    26\t| `zoom` | On date, time and number x only. A category axis does not zoom. |\n    27\t| `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n    28\t| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\n    29\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    30\t\n    31\tNot on a bar chart: `stacked` (that is `layout: 'stacked'` here), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n    32\t\n38:    code: 'AX101', level: 'error', title: 'A field that is not in the data',\n83:    code: 'AX104', level: 'hint', title: 'Numbers sent as strings',\n116:    code: 'AX106', level: 'warning', title: 'Values that are not numbers',\n120:    fix: 'Convert the values to numbers where the data is produced. For a field that is entirely plain decimal strings, use `parse: \\'number\\'` (see [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104)).',\n191:    code: 'AX111', level: 'hint', title: 'Instants that look like calendar dates',\n    66\tit('re-audits a copied void and grades the restored answer in Chrome', async () => {\n    86\t    ['primary', LA], ['primary', Tokyo], ['alternate', LA], ['alternate', Tokyo],\n   110\t    ['primary', LA], ['primary', Tokyo], ['alternate', LA], ['alternate', Tokyo],\n   118\tit('fails both the table and reader date when midnight is treated as an instant', async () => {\n   128\tit('accepts a day-first calendar format in the table and point reading', async () => {\n   136\tit('fails a sorted categorical line that bridges missing days even when its table matches', async () => {\n   145\tit('fails the plotted quarter order when categories stay in encounter order', async () => {\n   151\tit('fails when agent code filters out a valid revenue row', async () => {\n   159\tit('fails a wrong unit label on one series of a multi-series chart', async () => {\n   164\tit.each(answers)('fails when the %s answer hard-codes the primary values', async (task, source) => {\n   165\t  const primary = await readJson(taskFiles(task).fixture);\n   167\t    `render(element, ignored) { const data = ${JSON.stringify(primary)};`));\n   168\t  expect(result.timezones.filter(zone => zone.input === 'primary').every(zone => criterion(zone, 'table rows').pass)).toBe(true);\n   182\tit('fails a clipped visible title with an intact accessible name', async () => {\n   190\tit('accepts a title intentionally hidden with the documented option', async () => {\n   195\tit('fails a table with the wrong caption despite a correct figure name', async () => {\n   210\t])('fails a genuine omission when the note is changed with %s', async change => {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
