Z6gQHI7soUIjCDaI2m6IMRjGyWhEdlF11pLAYXObr8hFGYdrZ9HzQToaR1MIC/vc6hZLs6elnlH8Mq0\n   107\t{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011ChByc8DBrn1261PfmT8D6\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"\\\"Row getter\\\" refers to a JS getter property on a row object, not a field accessor option. I'll read the options reference to confirm what field objects accept\n   108\t{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011ChByc8DBrn1261PfmT8D6\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01QtQPDio68zaVqT6Ve1aZng\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/tmp/axylo-rehearsal-YDchIs/workspace/docs/options.html\",\"offset\":22,\"limit\":40},\"caller\":\n   109\t{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01QtQPDio68zaVqT6Ve1aZng\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"22\\t<h1 id=\\\"options-reference\\\">Options reference</h1>\\n23\\t<p>After this page you can look up any option of any chart type, any field option, and every member of the chart handle, with its t\n   110\t{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"037274e3-cc92-4f9f-9e50-c31045af83fe\",\"uuid\":\"9165f3ea-7ec0-4f08-8b7a-8c4b95de1e73\"}\n   118\t## Numbers sent as strings\n   119\t\n   120\tSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo looks at the whole field:\n   121\t\n   122\t- When every value is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`, and exponent notation such as `'1e3'`), the field is converted and drawn, and hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to say so with `parse: 'number'`. With that, there is no hint, and TypeScript accepts the string field on a value axis.\n   123\t- When the field also holds a number, or any string that is not a plain decimal (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`), or another kind of value (`true`), every string in it is rejected, plain decimals included. They are not drawn, and they are warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n   124\t\n   125\tThe conversion is to JavaScript numbers. Integers are exact up to 9,007,199,254,740,991 (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`); a larger one is rounded to the nearest number JavaScript can hold, without a diagnostic: `'9007199254740993'` reads 9,007,199,254,740,992. A string too large for a number (`'1e999'`) becomes infinite and is [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103). When exact counts could exceed that limit, check them with `Number.isSafeInteger(Number(value))` before charting.\n   126\t\n   127\t[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n    30\t    {\n    31\t      \"name\": \"table headers\",\n    32\t      \"pass\": true,\n    33\t      \"detail\": \"primary / America/Los_Angeles: Expected series columns [\\\"Visits\\\"], saw [\\\"visits\\\"]. | primary / Asia/Tokyo: Expected series columns [\\\"Visits\\\"], saw [\\\"\n    34\t    },\n    35\t    {\n    36\t      \"name\": \"table rows\",\n    37\t      \"pass\": true,\n    38\t      \"detail\": \"primary / America/Los_Angeles: 30 rows match. | primary / Asia/Tokyo: 30 rows match. | alternate / America/Los_Angeles: 30 rows match. | alternate / Asia/To\n    39\t    },\n    40\t    {\n    41\t      \"name\": \"plotted x order\",\n    42\t      \"pass\": true,\n    43\t      \"detail\": \"primary / America/Los_Angeles: Expected 27 left-to-right calendar slots, saw 27 stroke vertices. | primary / Asia/Tokyo: Expected 27 left-to-right calendar \n    44\t    },\n    45\t    {\n    46\t      \"name\": \"drawn gaps\",\n    47\t      \"pass\": true,\n    48\t      \"detail\": \"primary / America/Los_Angeles: Expected stroke breaks at 2026-03-06, 2026-03-12, 2026-03-26. | primary / Asia/Tokyo: Expected stroke breaks at 2026-03-06, 2\n    49\t    },\n    83\t<h2 id=\"validate-in-node-and-in-tests\">validate() in Node and in tests</h2>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"agent_message","text":"**Accept the batch as the DX12 release record for `1e93128`.** Both models passed 3/3 on each of four tasks, exceeding the owner’s 2/3 rule in every cell. I reconstructed the expected tables from the raw fixtures and matched all 96 saved Chrome tables, including the missing days. I found no materially wrong chart on either graded input. [DX12](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:234) · [Release rule](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/README.md:16)\n\n### What earned the passes\n\nTimes are original runner wall-clock seconds, rounded, in attempt order.\n\n| Task | What all six answers did | Codex | Claude |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Daily visits | Explicitly declared the UTC midnights as `type: 'date'`. Axylo sorted the 27 observations and broke the stroke on March 6, 12 and 26. `codex-daily-visits-2` showed lowercase “visits”, which the case-insensitive header check reasonably accepts. [Answer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-visits-2-UbIDoe/answer/chart.js:7) · [Grade](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-visits-2-UbIDoe/grade.json:33) | 48, 51, 58 | 78, 74, 70 |\n| Regional sales | Sorted a copy of the shuffled quarters and labelled three grouped series. Codex sorted the fixed-width quarter strings lexically. Claude parsed year and quarter. All declared `parse: 'number'`, although Axylo already converts these all-decimal-string columns. Removing `parse` in Claude’s control produced three AX104 hints. [Codex answer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-regional-sales-1-DNPmkQ/answer/chart.js:4) · [Claude control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-regional-sales-1-bUf5s8/transcript.jsonl:113) | 65, 63, 60 | 76, 83, 75 |\n| Signups | Read the actual key `signpus` and supplied the reader label “Signups”. The date-only strings work by default. All three Claude runs also tried `signups` as a negative control and got AX101. On the alternate input, Axylo showed the April 9 gap and disclosed one omission both visibly and in the accessibility tree. [Control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-signups-1-xUIhVW/transcript.jsonl:93) · [Grade](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-signups-2-kmTdYO/grade.json:48) | 46, 68, 47 | 56, 53, 61 |\n| Daily revenue | Explicitly chose calendar dates and converted five decimal strings alongside two numbers. Axylo supplied the date order and May 4 gap. This trap required agent action: Claude’s raw-data control still reported five AX106 omissions with `parse: 'number'`. [Control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-1-Rt4jil/transcript.jsonl:83) · [Expected readings](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/fixtures/daily-revenue.expected.json:10) | 65, 75, 122 | 115, 121, 81 |\n\n**Revenue is where a careful reviewer should qualify the pass.** In a separate read-only probe, all three Codex converters silently turned `\"0x10\"` into 16. All three Claude converters accepted `\".5\"` despite describing their regex as Axylo’s grammar. Claude revenue 2 and 3 also rejected the valid `\"+2\"`. Both graded fixtures use ordinary decimals. A revenue alternate containing these cases would let **table rows** catch wrong values and **reader note** catch silent conversions or spurious omissions. [Codex conversion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-2-HBZvJU/answer/chart.js:7) · [Claude conversion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-2-jtKCOV/answer/chart.js:3) · [Axylo’s grammar](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/values.ts:6)\n\n### What the agents read\n\nBoth families used **Getting started**, **Your data**, **Line** or **Bar**, **Options**, and the Node `validate()` guidance. Getting started explicitly teaches all five traps. Codex searched broadly across the index, conformance, stability, diagnostic pages and published examples, then reconstructed the options in its Node checks. `codex-signups-2` inspected the package manifest and both production and development bundles. `codex-daily-revenue-3` inspected the manifest and production bundle after Node rejected a permission flag. Claude read the package manifest in all 12 attempts, sometimes the README and `dist/index.d.ts` or `public.d.ts`, and often validated the same exported options function used by `render`. [Getting started](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md:41) · [Codex bundle checks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-signups-2-ORZvKV/transcript.jsonl:28) · [Claude declaration lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-1-Rt4jil/transcript.jsonl:125)\n\nThree documentation changes follow directly from the transcripts.\n\n- **Your data / Options:** Give the exact decimal grammar and a mixed-field normalization example that preserves invalid values for diagnostics. The existing example covers all-string data. `claude-daily-revenue-2` repeatedly searched `parse`, then investigated whether “row getter” meant a field accessor. [Data guide](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/data.md:118) · [Options entry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/options.md:136) · [Transcript](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-2-jtKCOV/transcript.jsonl:107)\n- **Published HTML, especially Options:** Make individual reference rows searchable as text. Long matching lines were omitted, and all 12 Claude attempts encountered a denied HTML-stripping command before continuing with file tools. Codex sales 2 and 3 searched literal closing tags to find the shared-axis `value` message. [Claude search result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-2-jtKCOV/transcript.jsonl:103) · [Codex lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-regional-sales-3-JikJY0/transcript.jsonl:30)\n- **Diagnostics / Node validation:** Put a runnable plain-JavaScript check beside the TypeScript examples and say explicitly that `validate()` returns hints in production too. Codex searched for the invocation. Claude sales 2 ran both builds and switched to a standalone `check.mjs` after its shell loop was denied. [Diagnostics](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md:59) · [Codex search](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-visits-1-k30Xl4/transcript.jsonl:18) · [Claude check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-regional-sales-2-9d6sKK/transcript.jsonl:97)\n\n### Re-audit and bias\n\nI replayed both audit versions on all 24 full transcripts. Both matched the saved records exactly. Eight passes stayed clean. Fifteen voids cleared: all 12 Claude runs, Codex visits 1, and Codex sales 2 and 3. The distinction is **execution**. Most flags were regexes, HTML tags or search text. Claude sales 2 and revenue 1 also named genuine `/tmp/axylo-tools` destinations in commands denied before execution. The sixteenth void involved a real lookup: `codex-daily-revenue-3` received “No such file or directory” for both `/AGENTS.md` and `/tmp/AGENTS.md` **during the attempt**. Counting that run was a retrospective eligibility change, recorded in its history. Claude revenue 1 also tried `git log` in its workspace and found no repository. [Denied helper](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-regional-sales-2-9d6sKK/transcript.jsonl:16) · [Missing-file result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-3-n1Cfgj/transcript.jsonl:6) · [Original verdict](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-3-n1Cfgj/run.json:232) · [Git result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-1-Rt4jil/transcript.jsonl:7)\n\nAll 24 input and grader hashes matched. Replay restored the saved answers for the 16 voids without another model turn. All 12 Claude charts, its one helper, and the Codex revenue-3 chart readback matched the transcript bytes. The other Codex file-change events record paths rather than file contents, so their provenance rests on the runner’s saved copy. I used the saved Chrome results and reran the read-only summary, whose final line was `rehearsal rule met`. [Restore and hash check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/reaudit.mjs:30) · [Replay decision](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/reaudit.mjs:69) · [Codex readback](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-3-n1Cfgj/transcript.jsonl:36)\n\nI found no model-specific cue or scoring preference in the prompts, fixtures or reader grader. The tasks closely follow the getting-started tutorial, the builder was one tested model, and the design reviewer shared its provider. The CLI conditions also differed: the original audit disproportionately voided Claude, the shell permissions differed, and only Codex reported effective `xhigh`. The record supports the release decision without a fresh batch. It cannot establish a model ranking or prove filesystem-enforced docs-only access. [Design review](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.md:1) · [Protocol and effort](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/README.md:12) · [Audit limits](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/README.md:26)\n\n### Copy for conformance and stability\n\nOn release candidate `1e93128`, Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5` each passed three uncoached attempts on each of four fixed line and bar tasks, for 24/24 after transcript re-audit. Each saved answer met the reader checks without error diagnostics on both inputs in Chrome 158 in the Los Angeles and Tokyo time zones. This agent rehearsal provides no estimate of general success and establishes neither human or screen-reader usability nor WCAG conformance."}}
